WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.490 --> 00:00:02.605
Cymbal's buckets are filling up.

2
00:00:02.625 --> 00:00:05.393
Raw supplier files, product images, and logs.

3
00:00:05.413 --> 00:00:07.165
All piled together.

4
00:00:07.185 --> 00:00:08.778
Nobody can find anything.

5
00:00:08.798 --> 00:00:10.178
That is not a data lake.

6
00:00:10.198 --> 00:00:12.050
That is a data swamp.

7
00:00:12.070 --> 00:00:17.125
One idea turns it around: a lake is Cloud Storage, plus discipline.

8
00:00:17.145 --> 00:00:18.737
Discipline has five parts.

9
00:00:18.757 --> 00:00:20.285
One: zones.

10
00:00:20.305 --> 00:00:23.223
Data moves from raw, to staged, to curated.

11
00:00:23.243 --> 00:00:25.670
Each zone is cleaner than the last.

12
00:00:25.690 --> 00:00:29.045
Two: open formats, like Parquet, Avro and Iceberg.

13
00:00:29.065 --> 00:00:31.470
Any engine can read them.

14
00:00:31.490 --> 00:00:32.832
Three: discovery.

15
00:00:32.852 --> 00:00:36.580
Dataplex finds the files, and registers them as tables.

16
00:00:36.600 --> 00:00:37.992
Four: security.

17
00:00:38.012 --> 00:00:41.865
BigLake tables give row and column security over files.

18
00:00:41.885 --> 00:00:43.690
So analysts query the table.

19
00:00:43.710 --> 00:00:45.725
They never get the whole bucket.

20
00:00:45.745 --> 00:00:46.937
Five: cost.

21
00:00:46.957 --> 00:00:50.910
Lifecycle rules move old files to colder, cheaper classes.

22
00:00:50.930 --> 00:00:56.610
The trap: giving analysts read access to the whole bucket, to hide a few columns.

23
00:00:56.630 --> 00:00:57.110
Recap.

24
00:00:57.130 --> 00:00:58.545
Five parts.

25
00:00:58.565 --> 00:00:59.095
Zones.

26
00:00:59.115 --> 00:00:59.945
Open formats.

27
00:00:59.965 --> 00:01:00.657
Discovery.

28
00:01:00.677 --> 00:01:01.820
BigLake security.

29
00:01:01.840 --> 00:01:03.780
And lifecycle rules.

30
00:01:03.800 --> 00:01:06.575
Try the checkpoint below.
